      "text": "First, let's find the side length of the square. Since the diagonal of a square is given by \\( s\\sqrt{2} \\) where \\( s \\) is the side length, we have:\n\\[\ns\\sqrt{2} = 5 \\implies s = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\]\nThe area of the square is:\n\\[\ns^2 = \\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\cdot 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12.5 \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, let's find the radius of the circle. The diameter of the circle is given as 5 inches, so the radius \\( r \\) is:\n\\[\nr = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\nThe area of the circle is:\n\\[\n\\pi r^2 = \\pi (2.5)^2 = \\pi \\cdot 6.25 \\approx 19.635 \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nFinally, the difference in the areas of the circle and the square is:\n\\[\n19.635 - 12.5 = 7.135\n\\]\nRounding to the nearest tenth, we get:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7.1}\n\\]",
