      "text": "To solve the problem, we first need to determine the side length of the square and the radius of the circle.\n\n1. **Find the side length of the square:**\n   The diagonal of the square is given as 5 inches. Let the side length of the square be \\( s \\). The relationship between the side length and the diagonal of a square is given by the Pythagorean theorem:\n   \\[\n   s\\sqrt{2} = 5\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( s \\):\n   \\[\n   s = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the area of the square:**\n   The area \\( A_{\\text{square}} \\) of the square is given by:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{square}} = s^2 = \\left( \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\cdot 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12.5 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Find the radius of the circle:**\n   The diameter of the circle is given as 5 inches, so the radius \\( r \\) is:\n   \\[\n   r = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5 \\text{ inches}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the area of the circle:**\n   The area \\( A_{\\text{circle}} \\) of the circle is given by the formula \\( A = \\pi r^2 \\):\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{circle}} = \\pi (2.5)^2 = \\pi \\cdot 6.25 = 6.25\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n   Using the approximation \\(\\pi \\approx 3.14159\\):\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{circle}} \\approx 6.25 \\times 3.14159 = 19.63495 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Determine the difference in area:**\n   The area of the circle is greater than the area of the square by:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{circle}} - A_{\\text{square}} \\approx 6.25\\pi - 12.5\n   \\]\n   Using \\(\\pi \\approx 3.14159\\):\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{circle}} - A_{\\text{square}} \\approx 19.63495 - 12.5 = 7.13495\n   \\]\n   Rounding to the nearest tenth:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{circle}} - A_{\\text{square}} \\approx 7.1 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square by \\(\\boxed{7.1}\\) square inches.",
